  <ferc:MannerAndExtentOfCorporateControl id="Tag472" contextRef="C1">Genesis Offshore Holdings, LLC - direct control Genesis Crude Oil, LP - indirect control Genesis Energy, LP - main parent</ferc:MannerAndExtentOfCorporateControl>
  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="Tag473" contextRef="C1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="text-decoration: underline; font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note 1 - Organization and Nature of Operations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;High Island Offshore System, L.L.C. (HIOS or the Company), a Delaware limited liability company, is a wholly-owned, indirect subsidiary of Genesis Energy, L.P. (Genesis), a publicly traded master limited partnership. Effective July 24, 2015, HIOS was acquired by Genesis from Enterprise Products Partners L.P. (EPD), a publicly traded master limited partnership.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;The Company owns an undersea natural gas gathering and transmission system, which includes the East Breaks Gathering System and three supply legs that connect to a 42-inch diameter mainline. The Company gathers and transports natural gas received from fields located in the Galveston, Garden Banks, High Island, West Cameron and East Breaks areas of the Gulf of America to a junction platform owned by the Company located in West Cameron Block 167. The Company provides gathering and transportation services as authorized by the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC). The Company's major customers include natural gas marketers and producers.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Note 2 - Significant Accounting Policies&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;These financial statements were prepared in conformity with the accounting requirements of the FERC Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than accounting principles generally accepted in the United States. The most significant differences between accounting principles generally accepted in the United States and the financial statements presented herein are; (1) FERC does not permit "push down" accounting for a purchase acquisition, (2) the presentation of removal costs collected from customers, and (3) the classification of certain accounts on the balance sheet and statements of income.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;In preparing these financial statements, the Company has evaluated subsequent events for potential recognition or disclosure through April 9, 2026.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Recent and Proposed Accounting Pronouncements&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;On January 1, 2023, we adopted the guidance under ASC Topic 326 Financial Instruments - Credit Losses. The standard changed the impairment model for most financial assets and certain other instruments. For trade and other receivables, held-to-maturity debt securities, loans, and other instruments, entities will be required to use a new forward-looking current expected credit loss methodology that generally will result in the earlier recognition of allowances for losses.&amp;#160; We have assessed our receivables for expected losses by considering current and historical information pertaining to our trade accounts.&amp;#160; The adoption did not have a material impact on our financial statements.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Cash Management&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;Genesis Crude Oil, L.P. (GCO) provides cash management services to the Company through a centralized treasury system. As a result, all of the Company's direct charges, allocated salaries and overhead expenses were deemed to have been paid by the Company to GCO, in cash, during the period in which the cost was recorded in the financial statements. In addition, all of the Company's cash receipts were advanced to GCO as they were received. As a result of using these centralized treasury systems, the difference between cash receipts advanced to GCO and the direct charges, allocated salaries and overhead expenses paid by GCO on the Company's behalf is reflected as a net change in advances to/from GCO as part of accounts receivable and/or payable to Associated Companies (GCO affiliates) in the Company's financial statements. In 2015, the Company entered into a Cash Management Agreement with GCO which specifies that the net Receivable from Associated Companies will generally be settled by recording an equity distribution to GCO, and as such, interest is neither paid nor received on the cash swept between the Company and GCO. In the event that the Company enters into inter-company debt with GCO, interest income or expense will be recorded. See Note 4 regarding detail of related party transactions. In accordance with the cash management policy, GCO has the discretion to deem the net Receivable from Associated Companies a capital distribution. In addition, GCO has the discretion of deeming any net Payable to Associated Companies as a capital contribution.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;The preparation of financial statements requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets, liabilities, revenues, expenses and disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities that exist at the date of the financial statements and the reported amounts of revenues and expenses during the reported periods. While the Company believes its estimates are appropriate, actual results can, and often do, differ from those estimates.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Utility Plant&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;Utility plant is stated at cost and includes all costs of construction and an allowance for funds used during construction. Depreciation of utility plant is computed on a composite or straight line method. Gains or losses on retirements that are significant or include an entire operating unit are included in income. All other dispositions or retirements are recorded as an adjustment to accumulated depreciation. Utility plant costs are only adjusted when utility plant is removed from service, sold or abandoned. Major improvements, including repairs or replacements of equipment that extend the useful life of utility plant assets are capitalized, while routine maintenance and repairs are typically expensed as incurred.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Allowance for Funds Used During Construction&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;In accordance with the accounting requirements of the FERC, an allowance for borrowed and equity funds used during construction is included in the cost of the Company's major additions to utility plant. During 2025 and 2024, no equity funds were capitalized. Capitalized interest, consisting entirely of an allowance for borrowed funds on the Company's construction projects, was zero in 2025 and 2024.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;Cash and cash equivalents represent unrestricted cash on hand and highly liquid investments with original maturities of less than three months from the date of purchase.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Fair Value Information&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;The carrying amounts of cash and cash equivalents, accounts receivable and accounts payable approximate their fair values based on their short-term nature.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Allowance for Doubtful Accounts&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;We review our outstanding accounts receivable balances on a regular basis and record an allowance for amounts that we expect will not be fully recovered.&amp;#160;We have assessed our receivables for expected losses by considering current and historical information pertaining to our trade accounts. Actual balances are not applied against the reserve until substantially all collection efforts have been exhausted. At December 31, 2025 and December 31, 2024 the allowance was $298,499 and $623,499, respectively.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;Our customers comprise natural gas producers. A performance obligation is a promise in a contract to transfer a distinct service to the customer. A contract's transaction price is allocated to each distinct performance obligation and recognized as revenue, when, or as, the performance obligation is satisfied. A performance obligation is distinct if the service is separately identifiable from other items in the integrated package of products or services and if a customer can benefit from it on its own or with other resources that are readily available to the customer. We had no contract assets or liabilities as of December 31, 2025 and 2024.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;We are subject to regulation by certain state and federal authorities, including the FERC, with revenue derived primarily from interruptible transportation contracts. Interruptible transportation agreements provide for a volumetric charge based on actual commodity transportation utilized in the period in which those services are provided. Our performance obligations for interruptible transportation contracts comprise an integrated package of services typically constituting a single performance obligation once scheduled, which includes receiving, transporting, and redelivering commodities.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;In situations where, in our judgment, we consider the integrated package of services to be a single performance obligation, which represents the majority of our contracts with customers, we do not consider there to be multiple performance obligations because the nature of the overall promise in the contract is to stand ready to receive, transport or store, and redeliver natural gas to the customer and therefore revenue is recognized over time upon satisfaction of our daily stand ready performance obligation.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;Revenues for commodity charges from interruptible transportation services are recognized based on volumes of natural gas scheduled for delivery at the agreed upon delivery point.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;Additionally, we operate certain lateral lines for producers that connect to our HIOS system and collect fees for our services. Our performance obligations for our operator and management fees comprise contractual promises to provide services over time and we recognize revenue when the performance obligation is satisfied.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Cash Distributions&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;Distributions are made from available funds at the discretion of GCO management. No distributions were made during 2025 or 2024&lt;strong&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;The Company is organized as a Delaware limited liability company and is disregarded for federal income tax purposes. Income taxes associated with its operations are the responsibility of its owner and would not generally be reflected in its financial statements. In accordance with&amp;#160;FERC&amp;#8217;s&amp;#160;Policy Statement on Income Tax Allowances (FERC's&amp;#160;Tax Policy), we eliminated the effects of income taxes as of the year ended December 31, 2017.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;Asset retirement obligations (AROs) are legal obligations associated with the retirement of tangible long-lived assets that result from their acquisition, construction, development and/or normal operation. When an ARO is incurred, we record a liability for the ARO and capitalize an equal amount as an increase in the carrying value of the related long-lived asset. ARO amounts are measured at their estimated fair value using expected present value techniques. Over time, the ARO liability is accreted to its present value (through an offset to accumulated provision for depreciation of utility plant) and the capitalized amount is depreciated over the remaining useful life of the related long-lived asset. We will incur a gain or loss to the extent that our ARO liabilities are not settled at their recorded amounts.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;Under the Company's FERC approved tariff rates, it is collecting from customers negative salvage amounts to be used to offset any future cost of removal obligations. These amounts are reflected in accumulated depreciation, depletion and amortization in the balance sheet. The amount collected as of December 31, 2025 and 2024 was $41.3 million and $40.2 million, respectively. The total collections, which will be received over time, will be used for any future removal costs.&amp;#160;The Company has recorded an ARO of $70.4 million and $66.8 million as of December 31, 2025 and 2024, respectively (see Note 7).&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: calibri;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Going Concern Considerations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;The Company has had negative cash flows and operating losses, and their ability to continue as a going concern is dependent upon full support from its ultimate parent company, Genesis Energy, L.P. Genesis Energy, L.P. has provided a letter stating its ability and intent to support the Company&amp;#8217;s operating, investing and financing activities through at least April 11, 2027. The financial statements presented within here do not included any adjustments relating to the recoverability and classification of recorded asset amounts or classification of liabilities that might be necessary if the Company is unable to continue as a going concern.&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Note 3 - Concentration of Credit Risk&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: calibri; font-size: 14pt;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;/u&gt;Financial instruments that potentially subject the Company to concentration of credit risk consist primarily of trade accounts receivable. The concentration of credit risk in a relatively small number of customers in the natural gas industry affects the Company's overall exposure to credit risk because the Company's customers may be similarly affected by changes in economic and other conditions. The percentage of total revenues from major customers were as follows for the years ended December 31:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
